not open to new ideas\". No explicit cooperation issues, but being a show-off may affect crew cooperation. No mention of communication issues.\n\nCandidate B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, weak memory for numbers, makes nasty remarks about colleagues. So reliability strong, stress strong, technical good, but communication/cooperation poor.\n\nCandidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not willing to further education, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology. So decision quality good, calm, technical, but communication and cooperation issues, plus lack of adaptability.\n\nCandidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as a know-it-all, has a hot temper. So technical, responsibility, but cooperation issues (know-it-all, hot temper). No explicit reliability issues.\n\nWe need to decide which candidate best fits overall. The new info about B's nasty remarks may tip the balance away from B. We need to consider if any other candidate has less severe cooperation issues. A has show-off and not open to new ideas, which could affect cooperation but maybe less severe than nasty remarks. D has hot temper and know-it-all, also problematic. C has difficulty communicating and egocentric, also problematic. All have some cooperation issues. Which is least? Possibly A: show-off and not open to new ideas, but no direct hostility. B's nasty remarks are direct hostility. So maybe A is better overall? Let's weigh.\n\nReliability: A no explicit reliability fact. B very conscientious (strong). C no reliability fact. D no reliability fact.\n\nStress resilience: A not mentioned. B handles stress well. C keeps calm in crisis. D responds adequately to unexpected events.\n\nTechnical competence: A sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision. B good at assessing weather, computer skills. C understands complicated technology. D solves problems extremely well, concentrates.\n\nDecision quality: C can make correct decisions quickly. A can anticipate dangerous situations (maybe decision). D adequate.\n\nAttention: D concentrates well. C maybe not.\n\nCooperation: A show-off, not open to new ideas (maybe less cooperative). B nasty remarks (bad). C difficulty communicating, egocentric. D hot temper, know-it-all.\n\nResponsibility: A leadership qualities. D takes responsibility seriously.\n\nOverall, B has strong reliability and stress, but communication is a serious flaw. A lacks reliability evidence but has leadership and technical. D has responsibility and technical but cooperation issues. C has decision and calm but egocentric and not willing to learn.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, cooperation and communication are crucial. Nasty remarks may be disqualifying. So I might shift recommendation to Candidate A, as they have leadership, technical, and less severe cooperation issues. Or maybe D? D's hot temper could be severe. A's show-off may be less severe.\n\nThus I will argue that after considering B's uncooperative behavior, I am less convinced by B and now recommend Candidate A. Provide evidence: B's nasty remarks, A's leadership and technical, and less severe cooperation concerns.\n\nLet's produce public message."}
